# The roll-aware arm IK (#304, D-050, F-212): the hand's roll about the forearm is shared along the arm
# instead of wringing the one band of faces at the wrist (research section 16a). The right controller is
# rolled 0 / 45 / 90 / 180 degrees hanging down and pointing forward - the eight poses of the #298 baseline
# (docs/vr/research/arms-roll.xrs, section 16g) - and status.json says where the roll went:
#   handRoll     the hand's roll about the forearm against the base bend plane (unwrapped past a half turn)
#   elbowSwing   how far the elbow swung about the shoulder-target line (the elbow share of handRoll)
#   residualRoll what is left against the final bend plane
#   forearmRoll  the forearm's roll off its bend plane (the forearm share of residualRoll)
#   upperRoll    the upper arm's roll off its bend plane (the upper share of forearmRoll)
#   wristTwist   residualRoll - forearmRoll: what the band between the forearm and the hand shows
#   elbowTwist   forearmRoll - upperRoll: the band at the elbow; the shoulder band shows upperRoll
# With the defaults (2/3, 1/2, 1/3, 45) a 90 degree roll leaves each band about 20 degrees and a 180 degree
# roll about 40 - against 90 and 180 on the wrist band with #36's rule. The joints are still met (error < 1).
# Needs the first-person save (level 10's conversation camera is nowhere near the body, TRAPS.md):
#   .\scripts\dev\xrs-run.ps1 arms-roll
#   .\scripts\dev\smoke-test.ps1 -VrSim idle -Sequence arms-roll -Save mvp-new-game
@sim idle
@save mvp-new-game
@args --loadslot 0

@until state eq GAMEPLAY 60s
@until vr.sim.timelineFrame ge 270 60s       # 3 s into the level: past its fade-in (TRAPS.md)
@assert player.controlsBlocked eq false
@assert vr.body.bound eq true
@assert vr.body.active eq true
@assert vr.body.right.posed eq true

# The shares in use are the defaults
@assert vr.body.forearmShare gt 0.6
@assert vr.body.forearmShare lt 0.7
@assert vr.body.upperShare eq 0.5
@assert vr.body.elbowShare gt 0.3
@assert vr.body.elbowShare lt 0.4
@assert vr.body.twistOverflow eq 45

# Look down-forward at the right hand in front of the chest, the left one off
vr-sim head rot 0 -40 0
vr-sim hand left off
vr-sim hand right pos 0.15 -0.3 -0.4

# A) the hand hanging down (fingers down): the forearm is vertical, so a yaw is a roll about it.
# The identity grip hangs the hand the way the model's rest pose holds it (palm to the thigh, thumb
# ahead), but this is not the rest relation: the arm hangs ahead of the chest and its bend plane is
# the pole's (down, back, a little out), 30 degrees from the hand's - the host test has the true rest
# relation at 0. So the roll is shared from here too: the elbow swung 10 degrees, each band under 6
vr-sim hand right rot 0 0 0
@frames 15
@assert vr.body.right.error lt 1
@remember down0HandRoll vr.body.right.handRoll
@assert vr.body.right.handRoll lt 40
@assert vr.body.right.handRoll gt 20
@assert vr.body.right.wristTwist lt 6
@assert vr.body.right.wristTwist gt -6
@assert vr.body.right.elbowTwist lt 6
@assert vr.body.right.elbowTwist gt -6
@assert vr.body.right.upperRoll lt 6
@assert vr.body.right.upperRoll gt -6
@shot down-roll000

# 45 degrees: the hand's roll moved against the rest pose - by less than 45 and the other way round
# (the forearm leans forward here, so a room yaw is about 0.7 of a roll about it, and a positive yaw
# unrolls this hand); the wrist band and the elbow band carry no more than their share
vr-sim hand right rot 45 0 0
@frames 15
@assert vr.body.right.error lt 1
@assert vr.body.right.handRoll lt {down0HandRoll-15}
@assert vr.body.right.handRoll gt {down0HandRoll-50}
@assert vr.body.right.wristTwist lt 25
@assert vr.body.right.wristTwist gt -25
@assert vr.body.right.elbowTwist lt 25
@assert vr.body.right.elbowTwist gt -25
@shot down-roll045

# 90 degrees: each band under 35 degrees (the whole 90 on the wrist band is the candy wrapper of #36)
vr-sim hand right rot 90 0 0
@frames 15
@assert vr.body.right.error lt 1
@assert vr.body.right.handRoll lt {down0HandRoll-40}
@assert vr.body.right.handRoll gt {down0HandRoll-95}
@assert vr.body.right.wristTwist lt 35
@assert vr.body.right.wristTwist gt -35
@assert vr.body.right.elbowTwist lt 35
@assert vr.body.right.elbowTwist gt -35
@assert vr.body.right.upperRoll lt 35
@assert vr.body.right.upperRoll gt -35
@shot down-roll090

# 180 degrees: a half turn, still shared - under 70 degrees per band
vr-sim hand right rot 180 0 0
@frames 15
@assert vr.body.right.error lt 1
@assert vr.body.right.wristTwist lt 70
@assert vr.body.right.wristTwist gt -70
@assert vr.body.right.elbowTwist lt 70
@assert vr.body.right.elbowTwist gt -70
@assert vr.body.right.upperRoll lt 70
@assert vr.body.right.upperRoll gt -70
@shot down-roll180

# Back to 0: everything returns, the overflow side is forgotten
vr-sim hand right rot 0 0 0
@frames 15
@assert vr.body.right.handRoll lt {down0HandRoll+5}
@assert vr.body.right.handRoll gt {down0HandRoll-5}
@assert vr.body.right.rollOverflow eq 0

# B) the hand pointing forward (fingers ahead, thumb up), rolled about the forward axis: 0, +90, -90, 180
# (yaw pitch roll compose as Ry * Rx * Rz: Rz(90) * Rx(90) = Ry(90) * Rz(90), Rz(180) * Rx(90) = Ry(180) * Rx(-90))
vr-sim hand right rot 0 90 0
@frames 15
@assert vr.body.right.error lt 1
@remember fwd0HandRoll vr.body.right.handRoll
@shot fwd-roll000

vr-sim hand right rot 90 0 90
@frames 15
@assert vr.body.right.error lt 1
@assert vr.body.right.wristTwist lt 35
@assert vr.body.right.wristTwist gt -35
@assert vr.body.right.elbowTwist lt 35
@assert vr.body.right.elbowTwist gt -35
@shot fwd-roll090

vr-sim hand right rot -90 0 -90
@frames 15
@assert vr.body.right.error lt 1
@assert vr.body.right.wristTwist lt 35
@assert vr.body.right.wristTwist gt -35
@assert vr.body.right.elbowTwist lt 35
@assert vr.body.right.elbowTwist gt -35
@shot fwd-roll-090

vr-sim hand right rot 180 -90 0
@frames 15
@assert vr.body.right.error lt 1
@assert vr.body.right.wristTwist lt 70
@assert vr.body.right.wristTwist gt -70
@assert vr.body.right.elbowTwist lt 70
@assert vr.body.right.elbowTwist gt -70
@shot fwd-roll180

# C) the roll past a half turn keeps its side until the overflow (RENIK's rule). The hand hanging by
# the side, so the forearm is vertical and a room yaw is a roll about it (a negative yaw rolls this hand
# positive; the hanging hand starts about 25 degrees from the model's rest relation, and a yaw step is
# about one roll step here): yawed on in steps to -170 the roll continues past 180 instead of
# flipping; at -215 it is past the overflow (225) and flips to the complement. The steps stay under the
# overflow: the simulated hand jumps, and a jump straight past 225 flips at once, as it should
vr-sim hand right pos 0.2 -0.55 -0.1
vr-sim hand right rot 0 0 0
@frames 10
@remember sideHandRoll vr.body.right.handRoll
@assert vr.body.right.rollOverflow eq 0
vr-sim hand right rot -100 0 0
@frames 10
@assert vr.body.right.handRoll gt {sideHandRoll+70}
@assert vr.body.right.handRoll lt {sideHandRoll+130}
vr-sim hand right rot -135 0 0
@frames 10
@assert vr.body.right.handRoll gt 140
vr-sim hand right rot -170 0 0
@frames 10
@assert vr.body.right.handRoll gt 180
@assert vr.body.right.rollOverflow ne 0
@assert vr.body.right.error lt 1
vr-sim hand right rot -215 0 0
@frames 10
@assert vr.body.right.handRoll lt 0
@assert vr.body.right.rollOverflow eq 0
@assert vr.body.right.error lt 1

@assert errors eq 0
